{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-80448d7015e141c5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tDo Text-to-Music Models Really Follow\n2\tInstructions? A Counterfactual Evaluation of Key\n3\tand Beat Grouping\n4\tYining Wang\n5\tBilibili Inc.\n6\t10inspiral@gmail.com\n7\t\n8\tarXiv:2608.11899v1 [cs.SD] 12 Aug 2026\n9\t\n10\tAbstract\n11\tPrompted attribute agreement is widely used as evidence of text-to-music controllability, yet a requested\n12\tattribute may occur simply because it is already common in the model’s output distribution. We introduce a\n13\tmatched counterfactual evaluation that separates target occurrence from instruction-attributable control. Each\n14\tfamily contains a neutral input that omits the scored attribute and two otherwise matched inputs that swap\n15\tthe requested target. All three are rendered through frozen native-interface adapters with a shared seed.\n16\tApplied to global key and beat grouping in three open systems, this design changes the empirical conclusion.\n17\tACE-Step 1.5 and Stable Audio 3 Medium exhibit substantial key control, whereas LeVo2 does not. For\n18\tbeat grouping, the same models redirect toward the rare three-beat target, but high four-beat agreement is\n19\tlargely inherited from neutral outputs: Stable Audio 3 produces four-beat grouping in 0.97 of neutral cases but\n20\tonly 0.56 under its explicit four-beat treatment. Off-attribute placebos, external recognizer validation, blind\n21\texpert annotation, and multi-seed sentinels support the attribution. When targets have unequal output priors,\n22\tagreement describes what a model produced, while matched neutral and target-swap contrasts test whether\n23\tthe instruction changed it.\n24\t\n25\t1\n26\t\n27\tIntroduction\n28\t\n29\tProfessional music production is constraint-driven. A generated passage may need to harmonize with an\n30\texisting arrangement, support a vocalist’s range, or sustain a recurring metrical organization through later\n31\tediting. Accordingly, recent systems expose key, meter, chord, tempo, and other music-specific controls in\n32\taddition to broad descriptions of genre and instrumentation Gong et al. [2026], Melechovsky et al. [2024], Lan\n33\tet al. [2024], Wu et al. [2023]. Whether these controls work is a practical question, not merely a prompt-matching\n34\texercise.\n35\tExisting evaluations usually answer that question by generating with a target-bearing prompt and checking\n36\twhether the target can be re-extracted from the audio. This prompted agreement criterion is necessary but not\n37\tsufficient. A system that already produces four-beat music in nearly every matched neutral context will often\n38\t“succeed” under a four-beat prompt even if the prompt contributes nothing. The output prior—often shaped by\n39\ttraining-data imbalance—is then credited as instruction following.\n40\tStable Audio 3 Medium illustrates the distinction. It produces four-beat grouping in 96.9% of neutral\n41\tbeat-family outputs, while the detected rate falls to 56.3% under an explicit four-beat instruction. Prompted\n42\tagreement still looks respectable in isolation, but the matched comparison shows that the instruction did not\n43\tcreate the apparent capability. For the rarer three-beat target, the same model moves from 1.6% under neutral\n44\tgeneration to 48.4% under treatment. The model is responsive, but prompted agreement alone assigns that\n45\tresponsiveness to the wrong target.\n46\tWe introduce matched neutral–A–B contrast families to make this distinction observable. A neutral input\n47\tomits the scored attribute, while two otherwise identical treatments specify different target values. The neutral\n48\tcontrast asks whether the instruction improves realization beyond the model’s context-matched output prior.\n49\tThe A/B swap asks whether changing only the target redirects the output. Frozen adapters render canonical\n50\tcases through each system’s documented public interface, so the estimand is the end-to-end behavior available\n51\tto a user rather than an architecture-specific internal quantity.\n52\tWe instantiate the design on global key and beat grouping across ACE-Step 1.5, Stable Audio 3 Medium,\n53\tand LeVo2. The evaluation comprises 256 complete families per system—192 for key and 64 for beat grouping—\n54\tand 2,304 first-pass outputs, with frozen sentinel families repeated under two additional seeds. Key provides a\n55\t\n56\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n57\t\n58\t1\n59\t\n60\t\fMatched prompts\n61\t\n62\tGenerate\n63\t\n64\tEvaluate\n65\t\n66\tBase =\n67\t\n68\tFrozen recognizers\n69\t\n70\t\"rock, guitar, bass, drums\" · 120 BPM\n71\tNeutral\n72\t\n73\tbase\n74\t\n75\tTarget A\n76\t\n77\tbase + \"in 3/4\"\n78\t\n79\tTarget B\n80\t\n81\tS-KEY\n82\t\n83\tMeasure\n84\tOccurrence: Is target present?\n85\t\n86\tP(target | treatment)\n87\t\n88\tkey\n89\t\n90\tshared\n91\tfamily\n92\tseed\n93\t\n94\tBeat This\n95\tgrouping\n96\t\n97\tbase + \"in 4/4\"\n98\t\n99\tlabels: yN̂ , yÂ , yB̂\n100\t\n101\tResponse: Did output change?\n102\tΔ = treatment − neutral\n103\tMargin = requested − alternative\n104\t\n105\tFigure 1: Counterfactual evaluation pipeline, illustrated with the open-text beat rendering. A matched neutral–A–B triplet shares one family\n106\tseed, is generated through a frozen system-specific interface, and is labelled by task-specific recognizers. Occurrence asks whether the\n107\ttarget is present; ∆ and Margin ask whether the rendered instruction changes and redirects generation.\n108\t\n109\tlow-neutral-prevalence regime, whereas three- versus four-beat grouping exposes a concentrated prior. This\n110\tcontrast reveals when agreement is informative and when it is not.\n111\tThe paper makes three contributions:\n112\t1. A prior-aware attribution framework. We separate requested-target occurrence from response beyond\n113\ta matched neutral output and from target-specific redirection.\n114\t2. A reproducible counterfactual benchmark. Canonical cases, frozen native-interface adapters,\n115\tcomplete-family inference, recognizer validation, placebo contrasts, expert audits, and seed sentinels\n116\tform one auditable pipeline.\n117\t3. An empirical account of structural control. ACE-Step and Stable Audio 3 show strong key control\n118\tand selective beat-grouping control, while LeVo2 shows little attributable response under its evaluated\n119\tinterface. Common four-beat outputs can appear controllable without a positive four-beat treatment\n120\teffect.\n121\t\n122\t2\n123\t\n124\tRelated Work\n125\t\n126\t2.1\n127\t\n128\tStructural Control in Music Generation\n129\t\n130\tText-to-music systems commonly condition on genre, mood, instrumentation, or style Copet et al. [2024],\n131\tEvans et al. [2024], Yuan et al. [2025]. More explicit musical control spans symbolic and audio generation.\n132\tFIGARO reconstructs symbolic music from descriptions including time signature and chords von Rütte et al.\n133\t[2024]; MuseMorphose controls rhythmic intensity and polyphony Wu and Yang [2022]; and MuseCoco links\n134\ttext to discrete musical attributes Lu et al. [2023]. For audio, Mustango/MusicBench enriches prompts with key,\n135\ttempo, chords, and beat information Melechovsky et al. [2024], while MusiConGen and Music ControlNet add\n136\ttime-varying chord and rhythm conditioning Lan et al. [2024], Wu et al. [2023].\n137\tThese systems expose different public inputs and are trained under different data regimes. Our aim is not\n138\tto attribute performance differences to architecture or interface class. We ask a narrower user-facing question:\n139\tgiven a released interface, does changing the requested musical value redirect the generated audio?\n140\t\n141\t2.2\n142\t\n143\tEvaluating Adherence\n144\t\n145\tMusicCaps, MusicEval, and SongEval emphasize text–audio alignment or perceived quality Agostinelli et al.\n146\t[2023], Liu et al. [2025], Yao et al. [2025]. CMI-Bench and ABC-Eval evaluate analysis of existing music rather\n147\tthan generation under structural constraints Ma et al. [2025], Zhao et al. [2025]. Attribute-level studies are the\n148\tclosest precedent. MusicBench re-extracts key, tempo, beat, and chord from generated audio. MusiConGen\n149\treports chord and rhythm agreement, FIGARO reports time-signature accuracy, and TPSMG reports key\n150\taccuracy Melechovsky et al. [2024], Lan et al. [2024], von Rütte et al. [2024], Zhu et al. [2026].\n151\tWe retain the condition → generation → re-extraction paradigm but change the comparison. Behavioral\n152\ttesting in NLP uses minimal pairs to associate response changes with controlled input edits Ribeiro et al.\n153\t[2020], while recent counterfactual-prompting work emphasizes that a textual edit bundles the target value\n154\twith its carrier and should be compared with benign perturbations Yang et al. [2026]. Generative music\n155\toffers an additional control: a matched neutral generation directly estimates the context-dependent output\n156\t\n157\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n158\t\n159\t2\n160\t\n161\t\fprior. Combining neutral and target-swap contrasts turns attribute scoring from an occurrence test into an\n162\tinterface-conditional attribution test.\n163\t\n164\t3\n165\t\n166\tCounterfactual Evaluation\n167\t\n168\t3.1\n169\t\n170\tMatched Contrast Families\n171\t\n172\tThe fundamental unit is a contrast family, not an isolated prompt. A family fixes genre, instrumentation,\n173\tBPM, output mode, decoding settings, duration, and seed. Its neutral member omits the scored attribute,\n174\twhile treatments A and B use the same template and specify different values. Figure 1 shows the open-text\n175\tbeat-family rendering; structured-field systems receive the same canonical contrast through native controls.\n176\tLet family f contain targets af , bf and outputs yf0 , yfa , yfb , and let s(y, t) ∈ [0, 1] score output y against\n177\ttarget t. We report treatment agreement,\n178\t\n179\tAccf = 12 {s(yfa , af ) + s(yfb , bf )},\n180\tand two attribution contrasts,\n181\t\n182\t∆f = 12 {s(yfa , af ) − s(yf0 , af ) + s(yfb , bf ) − s(yf0 , bf )},\n183\tMarginf = 12 {s(yfa , af ) − s(yfa , bf ) + s(yfb , bf ) − s(yfb , af )}.\n184\tFor target-specific analysis, let yft = yfa when t = af and yft = yfb when t = bf , and define\n185\t\n186\t∆f,t = s(yft , t) − s(yf0 , t),\n187\t1 X\n188\t∆t =\n189\t∆f,t .\n190\t|Ft |\n191\t\n192\tt ∈ {af , bf },\n193\t\n194\tf ∈Ft\n195\t\n196\tHere Ft = {f : t ∈ {af , bf }} is the set of families containing target t.\n197\tThe three statistics describe non-interchangeable operational properties. Acc. measures attainment:\n198\twhether the requested target occurred under treatment. ∆t measures enhancement: whether requesting\n199\ttarget t raises its occurrence above the matched neutral output. Margin measures differentiation: whether\n200\tswapping the requested value redirects the output toward the corresponding alternative. High Acc. without\n201\teither contrast can therefore reflect availability rather than instruction following, while a positive Margin with\n202\tnonpositive ∆t indicates differentiation without improvement over the neutral prior.\n203\tFor a confirmatory label of effective control, we pre-specify a two-part rule: the 95% intervals for both\n204\tneutral-relative enhancement and value differentiation must lie above zero. For beat grouping, where the two\n205\ttarget arms can cancel, the rule is applied to each target-specific ∆t together with the family-level Margin.\n206\tOutcomes that fail this joint rule are still reported by property rather than collapsed into “no sensitivity.” A\n207\tconservative two-sided family success rate and graded key variants are reported in Appendix A.4.5.\n208\tThe intervention is the rendered public-interface input, not an abstract sentence: for model m, a frozen\n209\tadapter Am maps a canonical case and condition T ∈ {0, a, b} to xm (T ). Neutral and treatment renderings\n210\tcan differ in both target semantics and their carrier tokens or fields. Consequently, ∆ is not an internal causal\n211\teffect of a latent musical attribute; it is the end-to-end contrast induced by the frozen user-facing rendering.\n212\tClaims of “control” denote this interface-conditional response under the benchmark context distribution and\n213\tdecoding protocol.\n214\t\n215\t3.2\n216\t\n217\tTasks, Cases, and Systems\n218\t\n219\tWe evaluate one clip-level structural condition at a time:\n220\t• Global key: one of 12 major or 12 minor keys. Exact tonic-mode agreement is primary and MIREX\n221\trelated-key credit is secondary.\n222\t• Beat grouping: modal beats grouped between detected downbeats, with three-beat and four-beat\n223\ttargets. This deliberately scores grouping rather than the notated denominator: 3/4 and 6/8 ambiguities\n224\tremain visible rather than being silently resolved.\n225\t\n226\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n227\t\n228\t3\n229\t\n230\t\fThe case matrix crosses four broad genres with task-appropriate instrumentation and BPM. Key families\n231\tbalance same-tonic mode swaps and tonic swaps across all 24 keys, while beat families contrast three- and\n232\tfour-beat grouping. Contexts that name the answer or lack sufficient pitched/rhythmic evidence are excluded\n233\tbefore generation. Chord progression, modulation, local edits, modes beyond major/minor, and irregular or\n234\tcompound-meter identity are outside the scored construct. The released benchmark contains no chord task.\n235\tThe primary systems are ACE-Step 1.5 Gong et al. [2026], Stable Audio 3 Medium Evans et al. [2026],\n236\tand LeVo2 Lei et al. [2026]. Each had executable public weights or inference code and could express both\n237\tevaluated attributes through a released interface. Canonical cases are rendered as documented fields, text,\n238\tor tags. Unsupported controls are never approximated. Table 1 summarizes the frozen input channels and\n239\tdecisive generation settings; Appendix A.3–A.4 provides literal renderings, checkpoint and repository identifiers,\n240\tfull decoding settings, output modes, durations, and seed handling. Mustango is retained as a short-form\n241\tauxiliary result in Appendix A because its native output window differs Melechovsky et al. [2024].\n242\tThe frozen matrix contains 256 families per primary system (192 key, 64 beat), three outputs per family, and\n243\tthus 768 first-pass outputs per system. A stratified 48-family sentinel subset (32 key, 16 beat) is regenerated\n244\twith two additional deterministic family seeds for every system. Main estimates use all first-pass families,\n245\twhile sentinels diagnose seed sensitivity without changing the primary sample. Appendix A.1–A.2 gives the\n246\tcanonical case schema, complete family allocation, target-pair schedule, and context palettes.\n247\t\n248\t3.3\n249\t\n250\tAutomatic Scoring and Inference\n251\t\n252\tS-KEY estimates major/minor key Kong et al. [2025]. Exact tonic-mode agreement is primary, and the MIREX\n253\trule gives graded credit to fifth, relative, and parallel relationships Raffel et al. [2014]. Beat This supplies beat\n254\tand downbeat events Foscarin et al. [2024]. Each complete interval between detected downbeats contributes a\n255\tbeat count, and the modal count with deterministic tie-breaking defines the grouping. Counts other than three\n256\tor four match neither target. Clips without sufficient valid events score zero under the end-to-end estimand.\n257\tThe primary estimand is the mean end-to-end family response over the frozen benchmark contexts,\n258\tconditional on the first-pass deterministic family-seed schedule. It is a paired, seed-conditional contrast rather\n259\tthan an average over an unspecified seed population. Sharing a family seed blocks generation noise across\n260\tneutral/A/B conditions, and shared family IDs provide the corresponding block across systems. Primary 95%\n261\tintervals are percentile intervals from 2,000 bootstrap resamples of complete families within genre, holding\n262\teach stratum size fixed (resampling seed 0). Between-system intervals use the same sampled family IDs in\n263\tboth systems for 20,000 paired resamples (seed 20260721). These are unadjusted per-comparison intervals.\n264\tSeed sensitivity is assessed separately on the three-seed sentinel subset. All first-pass outputs were valid, but\n265\tan invalid output would remain in this end-to-end estimand with score zero rather than being deleted.\n266\tTwo diagnostic controls target alternative explanations. First, the other task supplies an off-attribute\n267\tplacebo at no generation cost: key instructions are rescored for beat grouping and beat instructions for key.\n268\tThese are similarly positioned added music instructions, but they do not exactly match the target carrier\n269\tor tokenization. They test a generic added-instruction explanation rather than identify an internal attribute\n270\teffect. Second, three-seed sentinel triplets test whether the qualitative result depends on a single generation\n271\tdraw. Appendix A.5 indexes the frozen manifests, model and recognizer provenance, adapter renderings, and\n272\tanalysis code, and specifies the executable generation-to-analysis reproduction contract.\n273\t\n274\t4\n275\t\n276\tMeasurement Credibility\n277\t\n278\tThe benchmark is automatic, but its estimators are frozen and validated before generated-audio interpretation.\n279\tWe use complementary evidence: external real-music references establish operating ranges, blind expert\n280\tannotation checks generated material, and complete-triplet relabeling tests the family estimands themselves.\n281\t\n282\t4.1\n283\t\n284\tExternal Reference Validation\n285\t\n286\tKey recognition is domain-dependent but usable as a calibrated outcome measure (Table 2). GTZAN Keys\n287\toverlaps the evaluated pop, jazz, and rock styles, while GiantSteps adds a large independent electronic-music\n288\treference. Exact scoring is paired with MIREX because both human and automatic judgments concentrate\n289\terrors among musically related keys.\n290\tBeat/downbeat localization is strong across the reference sets, while the derived grouping decision varies by\n291\tdomain (Table 3). Errors on RWC Classical and GTZAN-Rhythm concentrate at metrical multiples—especially\n292\t\n293\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n294\t\n295\t4\n296\t\n297\t\fTable 1: Compact native-interface summary. “Base” is the frozen genre–instrumentation–BPM context. Slashes denote neutral / target A /\n298\ttarget B. The supplement contains literal inputs and complete provenance.\n299\tSystem / model\n300\t\n301\tKey rendering\n302\t\n303\tBeat rendering\n304\t\n305\tDecoding and output\n306\t\n307\tACE-Step 1.5 /\n308\t\n309\tnative keyscale: empty / A major / A\n310\tminor\n311\tBase / Base + “A major” / Base + “A\n312\tminor”\n313\tpure-music Base / Base + “A major” /\n314\tBase + “A minor”\n315\t\n316\tnative timesignature: empty / 3 / 4\n317\t\n318\t8 steps; instrumental; 30 s stereo, 48 kHz\n319\t\n320\tBase / Base + “3/4 time signature” /\n321\tBase + “4/4 time signature”\n322\tpure-music Base / Base + “3/4 time\n323\tsignature” / Base + “4/4 time signature”\n324\t\n325\t8 steps, CFG 1.0; instrumental; 30 s stereo,\n326\t44.1 kHz\n327\tBGM, CFG 1.5, temperature 0.9; requested\n328\t30 s stereo, 48 kHz\n329\t\n330\tacestep-v15-xl-sft\n331\tStable Audio 3 Medium\n332\tLeVo2 / SongGenerationv2-large\n333\t\n334\tTable 2: S-KEY validation on real music, frozen before generator scoring. Estimates are track-clustered; selection rules and per-genre\n335\tresults are in Appendix A.4.6.\n336\tDataset\n337\t\n338\tDomain\n339\t\n340\tGiantSteps via CMI-Bench\n341\tGTZAN Keys, fault-filtered\n342\t\n343\tEDM\n344\tMulti-genre\n345\t\n346\tN\n347\t\n348\tExact [95% CI]\n349\t\n350\tMIREX [95% CI]\n351\t\n352\t2,406\n353\t769\n354\t\n355\t0.506 [0.468, 0.544]\n356\t0.656 [0.619, 0.693]\n357\t\n358\t0.626 [0.594, 0.658]\n359\t0.765 [0.739, 0.790]\n360\t\n361\tfour-to-two half-bar estimates—rather than direct three↔four confusions. This pattern tends to reduce detected\n362\tfour-beat agreement rather than manufacture a target-specific treatment advantage.\n363\t\n364\t4.2\n365\t\n366\tBlind Expert Checks\n367\t\n368\tFive expert raters participated in blind protocols using one categorical instrument. A 48-clip real-music\n369\tcalibration set verified that single-clip key and grouping judgments operate in the same range as the automatic\n370\testimators. A separate 60-clip generated-audio audit, with three independent ratings per clip, found automatic\n371\tagreement with a stable expert majority of 0.808 exact (0.850 MIREX) for key and 0.643 for beat grouping.\n372\tPopulation reweighting produces 0.796 and 0.745, respectively.\n373\tClip agreement does not validate a paired estimand, so a further audit re-labeled 28 intact neutral–A–B\n374\tfamilies (84 clips) with one blinded bridge rater and recomputed the benchmark without changing its code. The\n375\tsample is split evenly by task (14 key, 14 beat), with 4–5 complete families per system. Because each beat\n376\tfamily contains neutral, three-beat, and four-beat members, both target arms are represented within every\n377\taudited beat family; genre cells contain 3–5 families per task. This allocation supports task-level and pooled\n378\tfamily contrasts, but not precise model-specific human rankings.\n379\tHuman labels preserve the key–beat separation and the signs of the family contrasts (Figure 3b): key ∆ is\n380\t0.286 by human labels versus 0.429 automatically, while beat ∆ is 0.036 under both. Family-level correlations\n381\tare 0.84 for key and 0.80 for beat. Thus the automatic pipeline is somewhat optimistic about key magnitude on\n382\tthis audit, but it preserves the paper’s contrast-level conclusion. Appendix A.4.7 and A.4.9 give the sampling,\n383\tambiguity, and calibration details.\n384\t\n385\t5\n386\t\n387\tResults\n388\t\n389\t5.1\n390\t\n391\tOccurrence and Control Coincide for Key\n392\t\n393\tFigure 2 places the matched neutral rate and treatment agreement on the same axis. For key, neutral agreement\n394\tis below 0.06 for all three systems because any particular tonic-mode target is rare without an instruction.\n395\tACE-Step and Stable Audio 3 then move to treatment accuracies of 0.667 and 0.674, yielding ∆ values of\n396\t0.612 and 0.646 and similarly large target-swap margins (Table 4). Their prompted agreement is backed by a\n397\tchange beyond the observed prior. LeVo2 remains near neutral (∆ 0.026) and has no positive target-specific\n398\tmargin.\n399\tMIREX scoring changes the credit assigned to related-key errors but preserves the separation: ACE-Step\n400\tand Stable Audio 3 obtain treatment scores of 0.763 and 0.739 with large neutral-relative gains, while LeVo2\n401\tremains close to neutral. Direct paired ACE−Stable intervals include zero for every key measure, so the\n402\tevidence supports two responsive systems rather than a key leaderboard.\n403\t\n404\t5.2\n405\t\n406\tBeat Control Is Target-Dependent\n407\t\n408\tAggregate beat agreement hides two different regimes (Table 4). Neutral outputs are overwhelmingly four-beat,\n409\tso high four-beat treatment occurrence largely reflects the default output distribution. ACE-Step changes little\n410\tunder a four-beat instruction, Stable Audio 3 decreases sharply, and LeVo2 remains near neutral.\n411\t\n412\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n413\t\n414\t5\n415\t\n416\t\fTable 3: Beat-grouping validation on real music. Grouping is derived by the same frozen modal-beats-per-downbeat procedure used on\n417\tgenerated audio.\n418\tDataset\n419\t\n420\tDomain\n421\t\n422\tBallroom, excluding Samba\n423\tRWC Classical + AIST\n424\tGTZAN-Rhythm v2, fault-filtered\n425\t\n426\tDance\n427\tClassical\n428\tMulti-genre\n429\t\n430\t○ matched neutral\n431\t\n432\ta Key\n433\t\n434\tΔ\n435\t\n436\tN\n437\t\n438\tBalanced grouping\n439\taccuracy [95% CI]\n440\t\n441\tBeat / Downbeat\n442\tF-measure\n443\t\n444\t602\n445\t37\n446\t619\n447\t\n448\t0.998 [0.994, 1.000]\n449\t0.734 [0.599, 0.867]\n450\t0.806 [0.678, 0.926]\n451\t\n452\t—\n453\t0.937 / 0.863\n454\t0.918 / 0.831\n455\t\n456\t● instructed (model color)\n457\t\n458\tb 3-beat\n459\t\n460\tΔ\n461\t\n462\tc 4-beat\n463\t\n464\tΔ\n465\t\n466\tACE-Step 1.5\n467\t\n468\t+0.61\n469\t\n470\t+0.25\n471\t\n472\t+0.05\n473\t\n474\tStable Audio 3\n475\t\n476\t+0.65\n477\t\n478\t+0.47\n479\t\n480\t-0.41\n481\t\n482\tLeVo2\n483\t\n484\t+0.03\n485\t\n486\t-0.02\n487\t\n488\t-0.05\n489\thigh prior\n490\t\n491\t0.0\n492\t\n493\t0.5\n494\t\n495\t1.0\n496\t\n497\t0.0\n498\t\n499\t0.5\n500\t\n501\t1.0\n502\t\n503\t0.0\n504\t\n505\t0.5\n506\t\n507\t1.0\n508\t\n509\tDetected target rate\n510\t\n511\tFigure 2: Target occurrence versus instruction-attributable response. Open circles are matched neutral target rates, filled markers are\n512\ttreatment agreement, and connecting segments and labels show ∆ vs. neutral. Key and three-beat gains are visible because treatment\n513\tmoves away from the prior. Four-beat agreement starts high, and Stable Audio 3 reverses under the explicit instruction.\n514\t\n515\tThe rare three-beat target reveals the controllable component: ∆3 is +0.250 for ACE-Step and +0.469 for\n516\tStable Audio 3, but unresolved for LeVo2. Under the fixed decision rule, the positive family margins localize\n517\teffective control to the three-beat arm for ACE-Step and Stable Audio 3. Neither system supports positive\n518\tfour-beat enhancement beyond its prior. For Stable Audio 3, the aggregate beat ∆ is near zero because +0.469\n519\tand −0.406 cancel, while the positive Margin shows that the two requested values nevertheless redirect output\n520\tdifferently. This is differentiation without neutral-relative enhancement, not total insensitivity to the requested\n521\tvalue.\n522\tPaired cross-model inference reinforces the target-specific interpretation. ACE−Stable is negative for threebeat ∆ (−0.219 [−0.359, −0.078]) but positive for four-beat ∆ (+0.453 [0.344, 0.563]). No target-independent\n523\tordering captures this behavior. The useful result is the response profile, not a single winner.\n524\t\n525\t5.3\n526\t\n527\tAlternative Explanations Do Not Reproduce the Pattern\n528\t\n529\tFigure 3 collects the most direct checks. Off-attribute placebos add a similarly positioned music instruction\n530\tbut no beat target. Their three-beat changes are between +0.003 and +0.008, far below the real +0.250 and\n531\t+0.469 effects for ACE-Step and Stable Audio 3. Stable Audio 3’s four-beat placebo drift is −0.060 [−0.109,\n532\t−0.013], again much smaller than the real −0.406 effect. These differences argue against a purely generic\n533\tadded-instruction explanation, although the placebo does not match the beat carrier or tokenization exactly.\n534\tAppendix A.4.4 and A.4.8 specify the sentinel and placebo analyses.\n535\tKey placebos expose a second distinction. ACE-Step preserves its neutral key under 85.9% of irrelevant\n536\tbeat-instruction perturbations but under only 9.6% of real key treatments, cleanly separating stability from\n537\ttargeted redirection. Stable Audio 3 changes key under both relevant and irrelevant text, so its large Margin—\n538\tnot movement alone—is the stronger evidence that changes land on the requested target. LeVo2 changes\n539\tmore under placebo than under real key instructions, matching its near-zero key Margin.\n540\t\n541\t5.4\n542\t\n543\tResponse Profiles Persist Across Seeds\n544\t\n545\tThe stratified sentinel repeats 48 complete families under three deterministic seed indices, yielding 432\n546\toutputs per system. Jointly resampling families and the three observed seeds preserves the key separation:\n547\tseed-marginalized key ∆ is 0.609 [0.515, 0.708] for ACE-Step, 0.698 [0.578, 0.807] for Stable Audio 3, and\n548\t0.010 [0.000, 0.031] for LeVo2. Stable Audio 3 ranks above ACE-Step on key ∆, Margin, and strict family\n549\tsuccess in each individual seed, while LeVo2 has zero strict key success in every seed.\n550\t\n551\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n552\t\n553\t6\n554\t\n555\t\fTable 4: Main first-pass results over complete contrast families (192 key and 64 beat families per system). Neutral and treatment columns\n556\tare occurrence rates. Beat ∆ is target-specific, and its family-level Margin is repeated across the two target rows.\n557\tNeutral\n558\t\n559\tTreatment\n560\t\n561\t∆ [95% CI]\n562\t\n563\tMargin [95% CI]\n564\t\n565\tACE-Step 1.5\n566\tStable Audio 3\n567\tLeVo2\n568\t\n569\t0.055\n570\t0.029\n571\t0.029\n572\t\n573\t0.667\n574\t0.674\n575\t0.055\n576\t\n577\t0.612 [0.563, 0.659]\n578\t0.646 [0.599, 0.693]\n579\t0.026 [0.003, 0.049]\n580\t\n581\t0.628 [0.570, 0.685]\n582\t0.635 [0.581, 0.688]\n583\t0.021 [−0.005, 0.047]\n584\t\n585\tACE-Step 1.5\n586\t\n587\t0.016\n588\t0.812\n589\t0.016\n590\t0.969\n591\t0.047\n592\t0.906\n593\t\n594\t0.266\n595\t0.859\n596\t0.484\n597\t0.563\n598\t0.031\n599\t0.859\n600\t\n601\t0.250 [0.156, 0.344]\n602\t0.047 [0.000, 0.109]\n603\t0.469 [0.344, 0.594]\n604\t−0.406 [−0.516, −0.297]\n605\t−0.016 [−0.063, 0.031]\n606\t−0.047 [−0.109, 0.016]\n607\t\n608\t0.398 [0.313, 0.484]\n609\t0.398 [0.313, 0.484]\n610\t0.328 [0.211, 0.445]\n611\t0.328 [0.211, 0.445]\n612\t−0.023 [−0.078, 0.024]\n613\t−0.023 [−0.078, 0.024]\n614\t\n615\tTask / target\n616\t\n617\tSystem\n618\t\n619\tKey (all)\n620\t\n621\tBeat: 3-beat\n622\tBeat: 4-beat\n623\tBeat: 3-beat\n624\tBeat: 4-beat\n625\tBeat: 3-beat\n626\tBeat: 4-beat\n627\t\n628\tStable Audio 3\n629\tLeVo2\n630\t\n631\ta Target-specific beat response ● instruction\n632\tACE-Step\n633\t\n634\tb Automatic vs. human labels\n635\t\n636\t○ placebo\n637\t\n638\t3-beat\n639\t4-beat\n640\t\n641\tKey\n642\t\n643\tAutomatic\n644\tHuman\n645\t\n646\tStable Audio 3\n647\t\n648\tLeVo2\n649\t\n650\t3-beat\n651\t4-beat\n652\t\n653\tBeat grouping\n654\t\n655\t3-beat\n656\t\n657\tAutomatic\n658\tHuman\n659\t\n660\t4-beat\n661\t-0.4\n662\t\n663\t0.0\n664\t\n665\t0.4\n666\t\n667\t0.0\n668\t\n669\tΔ vs. matched neutral\n670\t\n671\t0.2\n672\t\n673\t0.4\n674\t\n675\t0.6\n676\t\n677\t0.8\n678\t\n679\tΔ on audited triplets\n680\t\n681\tFigure 3: Specificity and measurement checks. (a) Filled markers show real beat instructions, while open gray markers show off-attribute\n682\tplacebo changes under the same family bootstrap. (b) Recomputing ∆ on complete triplets with blind human labels preserves the\n683\ttask-level contrast, with 95% family-bootstrap intervals shown by the error bars.\n684\t\n685\tThe beat profile is equally recognizable after marginalizing the observed seeds. ACE-Step has ∆3 = 0.208\n686\t[0.062, 0.375] and ∆4 = 0.042 [−0.062, 0.167]. Stable Audio 3 retains opposing target effects, ∆3 = 0.417\n687\t[0.250, 0.583] and ∆4 = −0.479 [−0.688, −0.271], and the sign pattern recurs in every seed. LeVo2 remains\n688\tunresolved for both targets. Thus generation randomness changes magnitude and some secondary rankings,\n689\tbut not the paper’s target-specific account.\n690\tA crossed family-by-seed decomposition attributes at most 0.03 of variance to a systematic seed-index\n691\teffect for almost every metric; the exception is LeVo2’s four-beat ∆ at 0.08. Family differences and familyby-seed residual variation dominate, so the sentinel should not be read as a universal seed average. It\n692\tinstead tests the qualitative claims under two additional draws without replacing the full first-pass matrix.\n693\tMeasurement-error sensitivity analyses likewise retain the observed beat target signs under Ballroom, RWC\n694\tClassical, and GTZAN-Rhythm transport scenarios. Appendix A.4.4 reports all per-seed estimates and the\n695\tdecomposition.\n696\t\n697\t5.5\n698\t\n699\tControl Is a Property of Targets and Contexts\n700\t\n701\tThe counterfactual view changes the granularity at which systems should be described. The key result is\n702\treplicated across four substantially different context palettes rather than being carried by one genre. ACE-Step’s\n703\texact ∆ ranges from 0.563 in pop to 0.688 in rock; Stable Audio 3 ranges from 0.521 in rock to 0.729 in\n704\tclassical. LeVo2 remains between 0.010 and 0.042. Genre changes the size of the response, but it does not\n705\tchange which systems exhibit large prior-relative key enhancement. This replication matters because genre\n706\tco-varies with instrumentation and BPM in the benchmark: the result survives those context changes without\n707\ttreating the four genre cells as separate leaderboards.\n708\tBeat response is organized more strongly by target than by genre. Each genre contains the same threeversus-four target contrast, yet its aggregate ∆ averages a positive rare-target arm with a weak or negative\n709\tcommon-target arm. For example, Stable Audio 3’s genre-level aggregate ranges only from −0.094 to 0.125\n710\teven though its pooled target-specific effects are +0.469 and −0.406. The small aggregate is therefore a\n711\tcancellation diagnostic, not evidence that the system ignored beat instructions. A response profile indexed by\n712\ttarget retains this behavior; a single system score removes it.\n713\t\n714\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n715\t\n716\t7\n717\t\n718\t\fInterface format, model architecture, and training data remain bundled in the released end-to-end systems,\n719\tso these results characterize public-interface behavior rather than isolating its cause. Detailed genre, outputmode, and sentinel breakdowns are provided in Appendix A.4.3, A.4.4, and A.6 as diagnostic maps.\n720\t\n721\t6\n722\t\n723\tImplications\n724\t\n725\t6.1\n726\t\n727\tFor Controllability Benchmarks\n728\t\n729\tPrompted agreement answers “did the target occur?” A controllability benchmark must additionally ask “did\n730\tspecifying it matter?” Matched neutral cases estimate the target’s prevalence under the same context. Target\n731\tswaps test whether the realized change follows the requested value, and off-attribute rescoring tests whether\n732\tthe effect is more than generic prompt perturbation. These contrasts require complete-family generation and\n733\tfamily-level inference, but no new human labels in the benchmark loop.\n734\tNeutral prevalence also determines the available headroom. For a binary target score, let p0t = Ef [s(yf0 , t)].\n735\tThe neutral-relative response is bounded by ∆t ∈ [−p0t , 1 − p0t ]. A target with neutral prevalence 0.97 has\n736\tat most 0.03 upward room but almost the full scale of potential deterioration; a target with prevalence 0.02\n737\thas the reverse asymmetry. This bound explains why the magnitude of ∆t should be read together with\n738\tneutral occurrence and Margin rather than compared as an unqualified effect size across targets. We do not\n739\tdivide by the remaining headroom: such normalization becomes unstable near one and would obscure a large\n740\tnegative response to a common target. “High prior” is therefore descriptive of the measured neutral rate, not a\n741\tthresholded label introduced after observing the treatments.\n742\tThis principle extends beyond music. Whenever a generative target is naturally common—four-beat\n743\trhythm, frontal faces, daylight scenes, majority dialects, or frequent code structures—conditioned accuracy can\n744\treward the data prior. Neutral-relative and target-swap comparisons identify the capability users actually need:\n745\tchanging an output on demand.\n746\tThe three-property report also changes how benchmark results can be used. Attainment describes whether\n747\ta target is available at all. Enhancement shows whether the released interface can increase it above a matched\n748\tdefault. Differentiation shows whether alternative requests lead to alternative outputs. A practitioner choosing\n749\ta generator may value any of these properties, but they answer different questions and should remain separate\n750\tin result cards. The joint decision rule is useful for a confirmatory claim of effective control; the full response\n751\tprofile remains the more informative diagnostic.\n752\t\n753\t6.2\n754\t\n755\tFor Music-Generation Models\n756\t\n757\tThe contrasts identify actionable failure modes. High agreement with small ∆ indicates an available target\n758\tthat the interface cannot reliably strengthen. Positive ∆ with weak Margin indicates prompt sensitivity without\n759\tconsistent target selection. Positive Margin with nonpositive ∆t indicates value differentiation without enhancement over a favorable neutral prior, as in Stable Audio 3’s four-beat arm. Low values on both indicate that\n760\tthe target is neither readily available nor responsive under the evaluated interface. These regimes motivate\n761\tdifferent interventions: data balancing and conditioning dropout for prior-dominated targets, stronger target\n762\trepresentations for nonspecific movement, or constraint-aware decoding when the attribute is represented but\n763\tnot maintained.\n764\tThe key–beat difference also cautions against a global “instruction-following” score. Key can be reinforced\n765\tthrough pitch-class evidence throughout a clip, whereas beat grouping depends on maintaining a beat/downbeat\n766\thierarchy. A model may control one without controlling the other, and may control a rare value within one\n767\tattribute more clearly than its common counterpart. Evaluation should preserve this structure rather than\n768\taverage it away.\n769\t\n770\t7\n771\t\n772\tScope and Limitations\n773\t\n774\tThe estimand characterizes end-to-end behavior through each system’s released interface. The resulting\n775\tclaims concern the evaluated systems under frozen prompting and decoding settings, rather than causal\n776\tattribution to architecture, training data, or interface design. Neutral prevalence is also protocol-conditional: it\n777\testimates the target prior under matched contexts, not a universal model distribution.\n778\tThe neutral contrast necessarily changes both the presence of a target and its rendered carrier, whether\n779\tthat carrier is text or a native field. The A/B swap is tighter because it keeps the instruction channel and\n780\t\n781\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n782\t\n783\t8\n784\t\n785\t\ftemplate fixed, while the off-attribute placebo only tests generic sensitivity to an added music instruction. It does\n786\tnot reproduce the exact carrier or tokenization. Our strongest interpretation therefore comes from agreement\n787\tbetween ∆t and Margin. A factorial extension crossing multiple carrier templates with target values could\n788\testimate carrier–value interactions, but the present benchmark does not identify a carrier-invariant internal\n789\teffect.\n790\tAutomatic outcomes inherit recognizer error. The large key responses remain well separated under exact\n791\tand MIREX scoring, external benchmark validation, and expert relabeling. By contrast, beat effects near zero\n792\tshould be interpreted as no detectable response at the resolution of the frozen estimator, not as evidence that\n793\tthe underlying effect is exactly zero. The complete-family human audit currently uses one bridge rater and\n794\tcalibrates task-level direction and magnitude rather than supporting per-model human rankings.\n795\tThe full matrix uses one deterministic family seed. A balanced three-seed sentinel subset tests whether the\n796\theadline response profiles persist across generation draws. The benchmark further targets global major/minor\n797\tkey and three- versus four-beat grouping. Modulation, local structural edits, and complete compound or\n798\tirregular meter identity remain outside its present scope.\n799\tNeutral omission is most natural for categorical attributes that can be left unspecified while preserving\n800\tthe intended generation task. Continuous controls, localized edits, and prompts whose task changes when\n801\ta constraint is removed may require an alternative reference condition, such as a neighboring value or a\n802\tdistribution-matched carrier. The general contribution is the separation of occurrence, enhancement, and\n803\tdifferentiation; the present experiment does not establish that one neutral construction is appropriate for every\n804\tcontrollability problem.\n805\t\n806\t8\n807\t\n808\tConclusion\n809\t\n810\tText-to-music controllability cannot be established by observing a requested attribute in isolation. Matched\n811\tneutral outputs reveal whether that target was already likely, and target swaps reveal whether changing the\n812\trequest redirects generation. Under this test, ACE-Step 1.5 and Stable Audio 3 Medium exhibit strong key\n813\tcontrol and effective enhancement of the rarer three-beat grouping. LeVo2 shows little attributable response\n814\tthrough its evaluated interface. The prevalent four-beat target demonstrates that high treatment agreement\n815\tcan coexist with no improvement—or a large reversal—relative to the model’s own neutral output. The\n816\tevaluation consequence is direct: agreement measures occurrence, whereas matched contrasts test\n817\tinstruction-attributable response.\n818\t\n819\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n820\t\n821\t9\n822\t\n823\t\fA\n824\t\n825\tBenchmark Implementation Details\n826\t\n827\tThis appendix gives the specifications needed to reproduce the benchmark and the diagnostics omitted from\n828\tthe main paper. Instantiated cases, prompts, configurations, predictions, and analysis outputs are indexed in\n829\tthe released code-and-data package.\n830\t\n831\tA.1\n832\t\n833\tA.1 Benchmark Instances\n834\t\n835\tEach canonical record stores the experimental condition independently of model-specific syntax:\n836\tFields\n837\t\n838\tRole\n839\t\n840\tcase_id, family_id\n841\t\n842\tCondition identifier and shared neutral–A–B family\n843\tidentifier\n844\tNeutral/A/B role and link to the shared neutral\n845\tTask and frozen target-pair schedule\n846\tFamily targets and active condition target\n847\tMatched context\n848\tEvaluation duration and repeat-subset membership\n849\t\n850\tcondition_role, comparison_control_id\n851\ttask_type, pair_slot, pair_index\n852\ttarget_a, target_b, target\n853\tgenre, instrumentation, bpm\n854\tduration_sec, sentinel\n855\t\n856\tThe primary matrix contains 256 families and 768 first-pass cases per system:\n857\tTask\n858\t\n859\tFamilies\n860\t\n861\tKey\n862\t\n863\t192\n864\t\n865\tBeat grouping\n866\t\n867\t64\n868\t\n869\tTotal\n870\t\n871\t256\n872\t\n873\tAllocation\n874\t24 keys × 4 genres × 4\n875\tbalanced pair/context slots\n876\t3- vs. 4-beat target pair × 4\n877\tgenres × 4 palettes × 4 BPM\n878\tvalues\n879\t256 neutral + 512 treatment\n880\tcases\n881\t\n882\tEvery key occurs twice as target A and twice as target B per genre. Pair slots M1 and M2 swap major/minor\n883\tat a fixed tonic; T1 and T2 swap distant tonics in a fixed mode. Tonic pairs exclude relative, parallel, and\n884\tadjacent-fifth relations, so cross-target MIREX credit is zero in those slots. The exact pairs are:\n885\tSlot\n886\t\n887\tPair construction\n888\t\n889\tOrientation\n890\t\n891\tM1\n892\t\n893\tp major–p minor, all pitch\n894\tclasses p\n895\tsame pairs\n896\tC–F#; C#–G; D–G#; Eb–A; E–Bb;\n897\tF–B, separately by mode\n898\tC–Ab; C#–A; D–Bb; Eb–B; E–F#;\n899\tF–G, separately by mode\n900\t\n901\tmajor=A, minor=B\n902\t\n903\tM2\n904\tT1\n905\tT2\n906\t\n907\tminor=A, major=B\n908\tfirst=A, second=B\n909\tsecond=A, first=B\n910\t\n911\tBeat families fix the context and compare no meter token, three beats between downbeats, and four beats\n912\tbetween downbeats. The adapter may render the treatments as 3/4 and 4/4, but the scored construct is beat\n913\tgrouping rather than notation.\n914\tFour instrumentation palettes are nested within each genre:\n915\tGenre\n916\t\n917\tPalettes\n918\t\n919\tClassical\n920\t\n921\tpiano + strings; string ensemble; piano trio; small\n922\torchestra\n923\tpiano + bass + drums; guitar + bass + drums; keys +\n924\tbass + drums; full pop band\n925\t\n926\tPop\n927\t\n928\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n929\t\n930\t10\n931\t\n932\t\fGenre\n933\t\n934\tPalettes\n935\t\n936\tJazz\n937\tRock\n938\t\n939\tpiano trio; guitar trio; piano quartet; vibraphone trio\n940\tguitar + bass + drums; dual guitars + bass + drums;\n941\tkeyboard rock band; piano + bass + drums\n942\t\n943\tTerms that reveal the target (major, minor, waltz, march) or weaken scorable evidence (percussion-only,\n944\tdrone-only, or monophonic-solo contexts) are excluded before generation. The released cases.jsonl files\n945\tare authoritative for the deterministic palette/BPM assignment.\n946\tQuantity\n947\t\n948\tPer primary model\n949\t\n950\tThree primary models\n951\t\n952\t768\n953\t288\n954\t1,056\n955\t\n956\t2,304\n957\t864\n958\t3,168\n959\t\n960\tFirst-pass outputs\n961\tAdditional sentinel outputs (replicates 1–2)\n962\tPrimary plus sentinel outputs\n963\t\n964\tMustango contributes a separate 768-output, 10-second auxiliary run. Preflight clips and development\n965\tpilots enter no reported estimate.\n966\t\n967\tA.2\n968\t\n969\tA.2 Native Interfaces and Generation\n970\t\n971\tCanonical conditions are rendered through frozen native-interface adapters: a structured field is omitted\n972\tfor neutral and filled for A/B; an open-text interface appends the same target template with value A/B; an\n973\tunsupported interface is marked unsupported rather than approximated. No recognizer hint is supplied to\n974\tgeneration.\n975\tThe following are literal renderings of one classical, piano-and-strings, 80-BPM context. Each condition is\n976\tshown on a separate line.\n977\tSystem and task\n978\t\n979\tFrozen rendered input\n980\t\n981\tACE-Step, key\n982\t\n983\tShared: caption = \"classical, piano +\n984\tstrings, 80 BPM\"; timesignature = \"\";\n985\tinstrumental = True; lyrics = \"\"\n986\tNeutral: keyscale = \"\"\n987\tA: keyscale = \"A major\"\n988\tB: keyscale = \"A minor\"\n989\tShared: caption = \"classical, piano +\n990\tstrings, 80 BPM\"; keyscale = \"\";\n991\tinstrumental = True; lyrics = \"\"\n992\tNeutral: timesignature = \"\"\n993\tA: timesignature = \"3\"\n994\tB: timesignature = \"4\"\n995\tNeutral: classical, piano + strings, 80 BPM\n996\tA: classical, piano + strings, 80 BPM, A\n997\tmajor\n998\tB: classical, piano + strings, 80 BPM, A\n999\tminor\n1000\tNeutral: classical, piano + strings, 80 BPM\n1001\tA: classical, piano + strings, 80 BPM, 3/4\n1002\ttime signature\n1003\tB: classical, piano + strings, 80 BPM, 4/4\n1004\ttime signature\n1005\t\n1006\tACE-Step, beat\n1007\t\n1008\tStable Audio 3, key\n1009\t\n1010\tStable Audio 3, beat\n1011\t\n1012\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n1013\t\n1014\t11\n1015\t\n1016\t\fSystem and task\n1017\t\n1018\tFrozen rendered input\n1019\t\n1020\tLeVo2, key\n1021\t\n1022\tShared: lyrics = '.'\n1023\tNeutral: [Musicality-very-high],\n1024\t\n1025\t[Pure-Music], classical, piano, strings,\n1026\t80 BPM\n1027\tA: [Musicality-very-high], [Pure-Music],\n1028\tclassical, piano, strings, 80 BPM, A major\n1029\tB: [Musicality-very-high], [Pure-Music],\n1030\tclassical, piano, strings, 80 BPM, A minor\n1031\tShared: lyrics = '.'\n1032\tNeutral: [Musicality-very-high],\n1033\t[Pure-Music], classical, piano, strings,\n1034\t80 BPM\n1035\tA: [Musicality-very-high], [Pure-Music],\n1036\tclassical, piano, strings, 80 BPM, 3/4\n1037\ttime signature\n1038\tB: [Musicality-very-high], [Pure-Music],\n1039\tclassical, piano, strings, 80 BPM, 4/4\n1040\ttime signature\n1041\tNeutral: A classical instrumental piece\n1042\tfeaturing piano, strings at 80 beats per\n1043\tminute.\n1044\tA: A classical instrumental piece\n1045\tfeaturing piano, strings at 80 beats per\n1046\tminute. The key is A major.\n1047\tB: A classical instrumental piece\n1048\tfeaturing piano, strings at 80 beats per\n1049\tminute. The key is A minor.\n1050\tNeutral: A classical instrumental piece\n1051\tfeaturing piano, strings at 80 beats per\n1052\tminute.\n1053\tA: A classical instrumental piece\n1054\tfeaturing piano, strings at 80 beats per\n1055\tminute. The time signature is 3/4.\n1056\tB: A classical instrumental piece\n1057\tfeaturing piano, strings at 80 beats per\n1058\tminute. The time signature is 4/4.\n1059\t\n1060\tLeVo2, beat\n1061\t\n1062\tMustango, key\n1063\t\n1064\tMustango, beat\n1065\t\n1066\tACE-Step uses the documented Unicode flat sign in its key field; canonical IDs use ASCII spellings. Stable\n1067\tAudio 3 and LeVo2 share the same open-prompt template, with LeVo2 adding its pure-music prefix and\n1068\tcomma-separated instruments.\n1069\tSystem\n1070\t\n1071\tFrozen model\n1072\t\n1073\tDecoding\n1074\t\n1075\tOutput\n1076\t\n1077\tACE-Step 1.5\n1078\t\n1079\tDiT\n1080\tacestep-v15-xl-sft;\n1081\tLM acestep-5Hz-lm-4B\n1082\t\n1083\t30 s, stereo, 48 kHz\n1084\t\n1085\tStable Audio 3 Medium\n1086\t\n1087\trepository revision\n1088\t5866e5b415a2\n1089\t\n1090\tLeVo2\n1091\t\n1092\tSongGeneration-v2large; revision\n1093\t\n1094\t8 steps; DiT guidance\n1095\t7.0; Euler ODE; LM\n1096\ttemperature 0.85, CFG\n1097\t2.0, top-p 0.9;\n1098\tthinking=True; batch 1\n1099\t8 steps; CFG 1.0; half\n1100\tprecision; batch 1;\n1101\tchunked decode\n1102\tBGM; CFG 1.5;\n1103\ttemperature 0.9; top-k\n1104\t50; top-p 0; stride 5;\n1105\tchunked decode\n1106\t\n1107\t653cbcf47161\n1108\t\n1109\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n1110\t\n1111\t12\n1112\t\n1113\t30 s, stereo, 44.1 kHz\n1114\t\n1115\trequested 30 s, stereo,\n1116\t48 kHz\n1117\t\n1118\t\fSystem\n1119\t\n1120\tFrozen model\n1121\t\n1122\tDecoding\n1123\t\n1124\tOutput\n1125\t\n1126\tMustango (auxiliary)\n1127\t\n1128\tdeclare-lab/mustango;\n1129\tscheduler\n1130\t\n1131\t100 steps; guidance 3.0\n1132\t\n1133\t10 s, 16 kHz\n1134\t\n1135\tbb2154823665\n1136\tFor family f and replicate r , local systems use the 31-bit seed\n1137\t\n1138\tseed(f, r) = int(SHA256(f : r), 16) mod 231 .\n1139\tNeutral, A, and B share this seed. Replicate 0 is the full-matrix run; sentinel replicates use r = 1, 2.\n1140\tRandom states are reset immediately before generation when the public interface lacks a seed argument.\n1141\tEach manifest records case, replicate, seed, status, audio path, and elapsed time; open-text systems also\n1142\trecord the rendered prompt.\n1143\tEvaluation uses the first 30 seconds after detected musical onset. A 15–30-second remainder is scored\n1144\twithout looping or padding; less than 15 seconds or no onset is an invalid generation and receives zero.\n1145\tACE-Step and Stable Audio use instrumental mode. LeVo2 uses its released pure-music rendering with\n1146\tlyrics='.'.\n1147\t\n1148\tA.3\n1149\t\n1150\tA.3 Scoring and Statistical Inference\n1151\t\n1152\tThe main paper defines treatment agreement, ∆f , and Margin. We additionally report\n1153\t\n1154\tStrictf = 1{s(yfA , A) > s(yfA , B)}1{s(yfB , B) > s(yfB , A)}.\n1155\tIts mean is Strict Acc.; a tie or one-sided success fails. Invalid generations score zero for both targets, and\n1156\tincomplete families are not dropped.\n1157\tThe primary estimand averages conditional output response over the frozen family/context distribution and\n1158\treplicate-0 seed schedule. Between-model contrasts are paired over shared family IDs. The sentinel analysis\n1159\tseparately averages over the finite observed replicate set r ∈ {0, 1, 2} on 48 frozen families.\n1160\tFirst-pass intervals use 2,000 percentile bootstrap draws (RNG seed 0), resampling complete families\n1161\twithin task×genre strata. Direct model differences use 20,000 draws (seed 20260721) and reuse sampled\n1162\tfamily IDs across models. Sentinel intervals use 10,000 draws (seed 20260721); seed-marginalized draws\n1163\tresample three replicate indices and then complete families within genre, with the same indices and families\n1164\treused for paired model comparisons. No multiplicity correction is applied to diagnostic target, genre, or\n1165\tsentinel breakdowns.\n1166\tAcc. is descriptive. Effective control requires 95% intervals for both ∆ and Margin to lie above zero. For\n1167\tbeat grouping, where target arms may cancel, the rule is applied to target-specific ∆t together with the family\n1168\tMargin. Positive Margin without a positive ∆t establishes value differentiation, not improvement beyond the\n1169\tneutral prior.\n1170\tA.3.1\n1171\t\n1172\tA.3.1 Key and Beat Scoring\n1173\t\n1174\tKey uses exact 24-class tonic-mode agreement as primary. MIREX gives 1.0 to exact/enharmonic matches,\n1175\t0.5 to fifth relationships in either direction, 0.3 to relative keys, 0.2 to parallel keys, and 0 otherwise [Raffel\n1176\tet al., 2014]. Relationships are tested in that order.\n1177\tFor beat grouping, Beat This supplies beat and downbeat events [Foscarin et al., 2024]. Each interval\n1178\t[di , di+1 ) contributes the number of beats in [di − 0.01, di+1 − 0.01). Empty intervals are discarded; the modal\n1179\tcount over retained intervals is the estimate, with ties resolved by first temporal occurrence. Counts other than\n1180\t3 or 4 match neither target. Fewer than two downbeats or no retained interval yields explicit detection failure\n1181\tand score zero.\n1182\tPair-stratified MIREX margins avoid pooling alternatives with different built-in relationship credit:\n1183\tSystem\n1184\tACE-Step 1.5\n1185\tStable Audio 3 Medium\n1186\tLeVo2\n1187\t\n1188\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n1189\t\n1190\tParallel major/minor [95% CI]\n1191\t\n1192\tDistant same-mode [95% CI]\n1193\t\n1194\t0.493 [0.419, 0.560]\n1195\t0.474 [0.395, 0.545]\n1196\t0.019 [−0.005, 0.044]\n1197\t\n1198\t0.730 [0.677, 0.783]\n1199\t0.715 [0.665, 0.768]\n1200\t0.007 [−0.038, 0.053]\n1201\t\n1202\t13\n1203\t\n1204\t\fA.4\n1205\t\n1206\tA.4 Measurement Validation\n1207\t\n1208\tExternal references are scored by the same frozen procedures used for generated audio:\n1209\tDataset\n1210\t\n1211\tDomain and retained N\n1212\t\n1213\tPrimary result [95% CI]\n1214\t\n1215\tGiantSteps via\n1216\tCMI-Bench\n1217\t\n1218\tEDM clips, 2,406 (604\n1219\ttracks)\n1220\t\n1221\tGTZAN Keys,\n1222\tfault-filtered [O’Brien\n1223\tand Lerch, 2015,\n1224\tSturm, 2013]\n1225\tBallroom, excluding\n1226\tSamba\n1227\tRWC Classical +\n1228\tAIST\n1229\t\n1230\tmulti-genre tracks, 769\n1231\t\n1232\tmacro exact 0.506 [0.468,\n1233\t0.544]; MIREX 0.626 [0.594,\n1234\t0.658]\n1235\tmacro exact 0.656 [0.619,\n1236\t0.693]; MIREX 0.765 [0.739,\n1237\t0.790]\n1238\t\n1239\ttrack-clustered;\n1240\tclass/mode\n1241\timbalance\n1242\tjazz/pop/rock exact\n1243\t0.478/0.821/0.663\n1244\t\n1245\tbalanced grouping 0.998\n1246\t[0.994, 1.000]\n1247\tbalanced grouping 0.734\n1248\t[0.599, 0.867]\n1249\t\n1250\t173 triple, 429\n1251\tquadruple\n1252\tBeat / Downbeat\n1253\tF-measure: 0.937 /\n1254\t0.863; 18 triple, 19\n1255\tquadruple\n1256\tBeat / Downbeat\n1257\tF-measure: 0.918 /\n1258\t0.831\n1259\t\n1260\tGTZAN-Rhythm v2,\n1261\tfault-filtered\n1262\t[Marchand et al.,\n1263\t2015, Sturm, 2013]\n1264\t\n1265\tdance recordings, 602\n1266\tstable 30-s windows, 37\n1267\t\n1268\tmulti-genre tracks, 619\n1269\t\n1270\tbalanced grouping 0.806\n1271\t[0.678, 0.926]\n1272\t\n1273\tDiagnostic\n1274\t\n1275\tGTZAN key labels marked unknown are excluded, and the published fault filter addresses documented\n1276\trepetitions and distortions. RWC and GTZAN-Rhythm retain tracks whose modal annotated grouping is 3 or 4\n1277\tin at least 90% of complete bars. Their grouping errors concentrate at metrical multiples, especially 4 → 2,\n1278\trather than direct 3 ↔ 4 confusion. Synthetic cadences cover all 24 key classes; click, one-bar, and silence\n1279\tprobes test detector failure behavior. The beat detector now returns explicit failure rather than defaulting\n1280\tinsufficient evidence to 4/4; this branch was not invoked by any of the 768 generated beat clips.\n1281\tA frozen-runtime repeat of 53 cases agreed across all three evaluations. Five historical generated-beat\n1282\tlabels changed under the frozen current runtime; ACE-Step and Stable Audio metrics were unchanged, while\n1283\tLeVo2’s beat Acc./∆/Margin changed from 0.445/−0.031/−0.023 to 0.453/−0.016/−0.016. Original caches\n1284\tremain separate from corrected sensitivity outputs.\n1285\tTransporting the Ballroom, RWC, and GTZAN-Rhythm confusion matrices to Stable Audio 3 gives aggregate\n1286\tbeat ∆ estimates of 0.030, −0.087, and 0.024, with intervals [−0.050, 0.110], [−0.268, 0.084], and [−0.183,\n1287\t0.221]. In every scenario the corrected 3-beat response remains positive and the 4-beat response negative.\n1288\tClass-wise key inversion is unstable when a one-vs.-rest Youden index approaches zero, so we report validation\n1289\tperformance rather than a de-attenuated key point estimate.\n1290\tA.4.1\n1291\t\n1292\tA.4.1 Human Validation\n1293\t\n1294\tFive expert raters used one blinded categorical instrument. The primary generated-audio audit contains 30 key\n1295\tand 30 beat clips, with three independent ratings per clip. Sampling is stratified over model×genre×automaticoutcome cells and reweighted to the full primary population. Ambiguous or split human panels remain\n1296\tambiguous rather than being forced into a class.\n1297\tComparison\n1298\tStable-majority rate,\n1299\treweighted\n1300\tAuto vs. stable majority,\n1301\treweighted exact\n1302\tAuto vs. majority,\n1303\treweighted joint exact\n1304\t\n1305\tKey\n1306\t\n1307\tBeat grouping\n1308\t\n1309\t0.888 [0.796, 0.980]\n1310\t\n1311\t0.889 [0.778, 1.000]\n1312\t\n1313\t0.796 [0.664, 0.916]\n1314\t\n1315\t0.745 [0.671, 0.829]\n1316\t\n1317\t0.707 [0.562, 0.854]\n1318\t\n1319\t0.662 [0.578, 0.747]\n1320\t\n1321\tA follow-up audit relabels 28 intact neutral–A–B families (84 clips; 14 families per task) with one blinded\n1322\tbridge rater and recomputes the benchmark using the same estimand code. Clip-level exact agreement given\n1323\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n1324\t\n1325\t14\n1326\t\n1327\t\fa stable label is 0.711 [0.552, 0.830] for key and 0.795 [0.645, 0.892] for beat. Human−automatic differences\n1328\tare −0.107 [−0.214, −0.036] for key Acc. and −0.143 [−0.250, −0.036] for key ∆; beat Acc., ∆, and Strict\n1329\tAcc. differences are zero, and beat Margin differs by +0.036 [−0.143, 0.214]. Positive-versus-nonpositive\n1330\tfamily direction agrees in 0.857–1.000 of cases across metrics. These small single-rater model cells support\n1331\ttask-level calibration, not human model ranking.\n1332\tCAL48 contains 24 key clips from fault-filtered GTZAN Keys and 24 balanced triple/quadruple windows\n1333\tfrom RWC Classical. Two blind raters achieve key exact/MIREX agreement with references of 0.500/0.642 and\n1334\t0.542/0.679; beat exact agreement is 0.792 and 0.750. On generated audio, a stable majority exists for 26/30\n1335\tkey and 28/30 beat clips (Fleiss κ 0.69 and 0.45). Beat disagreements are dominated by metrical-multiple splits\n1336\tsuch as {2,4} and {3,6}; key disagreements receive higher MIREX than exact agreement, indicating related-key\n1337\tambiguity.\n1338\t\n1339\tA.5\n1340\t\n1341\tA.5 Robustness and Heterogeneity\n1342\t\n1343\tA.5.1\n1344\t\n1345\tA.5.1 Three-Seed Sentinel\n1346\t\n1347\tA metadata-frozen subset of 32 key and 16 beat families receives two additional complete triplets for every\n1348\tprimary system. Key has eight families per genre and per pair slot, with each BPM and palette occurring\n1349\ttwice per genre. Beat has four families per genre, matched one-to-one over four BPMs and four palettes. A\n1350\tdeterministic metadata-only tie-break selects among quota-satisfying subsets. Sentinel cases are identical\n1351\tacross systems and do not increase the primary sample size.\n1352\tOne LeVo2 replicate-2 treatment was shorter than 15 seconds and is scored as a generation failure.\n1353\tExcluding that family symmetrically changes no estimate by more than 0.02.\n1354\tTable A.1: Three-seed estimates on the frozen sentinel. Intervals jointly resample the three observed\n1355\treplicate indices and complete families within genre.\n1356\t\n1357\tModel\n1358\t\n1359\tTask\n1360\t\n1361\tAcc. [95% CI]\n1362\t\n1363\t∆ [95% CI]\n1364\t\n1365\tMargin [95% CI]\n1366\t\n1367\tACE-Step\n1368\t1.5\n1369\tStable Audio\n1370\t3\n1371\tLeVo2\n1372\t\n1373\tKey\n1374\t\n1375\t0.651 [0.557,\n1376\t0.750]\n1377\t0.734 [0.625,\n1378\t0.833]\n1379\t0.021 [0.000,\n1380\t0.047]\n1381\t0.531 [0.427,\n1382\t0.625]\n1383\t0.448 [0.323,\n1384\t0.573]\n1385\t0.469 [0.417,\n1386\t0.500]\n1387\t\n1388\t0.609 [0.515,\n1389\t0.708]\n1390\t0.698 [0.578,\n1391\t0.807]\n1392\t0.010 [0.000,\n1393\t0.031]\n1394\t0.125 [0.052,\n1395\t0.208]\n1396\t−0.031 [−0.167,\n1397\t0.104]\n1398\t0.000 [−0.073,\n1399\t0.083]\n1400\t\n1401\t0.599 [0.479,\n1402\t0.719]\n1403\t0.714 [0.594,\n1404\t0.818]\n1405\t−0.005 [−0.042,\n1406\t0.031]\n1407\t0.385 [0.260,\n1408\t0.500]\n1409\t0.188 [−0.010,\n1410\t0.375]\n1411\t0.031 [−0.052,\n1412\t0.115]\n1413\t\n1414\tACE-Step\n1415\t1.5\n1416\tStable Audio\n1417\t3\n1418\tLeVo2\n1419\t\n1420\tKey\n1421\tKey\n1422\tBeat\n1423\tBeat\n1424\tBeat\n1425\t\n1426\tStrict Acc. [95%\n1427\tCI]\n1428\t0.448 [0.292,\n1429\t0.604]\n1430\t0.562 [0.417,\n1431\t0.708]\n1432\t0.000\n1433\t0.146 [0.042,\n1434\t0.271]\n1435\t0.167 [0.021,\n1436\t0.354]\n1437\t0.000\n1438\t\n1439\tTarget-specific three-seed beat ∆ values are: ACE-Step, 3-beat 0.208 [0.062, 0.375] and 4-beat 0.042\n1440\t[−0.062, 0.167]; Stable Audio 3, 0.417 [0.250, 0.583] and −0.479 [−0.688, −0.271]; LeVo2, −0.021 [−0.083,\n1441\t0.000] and 0.021 [−0.125, 0.188]. Stable Audio 3’s opposing target signs recur in every seed. The key\n1442\t∆/Margin/Strict ordering Stable Audio 3 > ACE-Step > LeVo2 also recurs in every seed; beat Strict Acc. swaps\n1443\tthe top two systems only in replicate 2.\n1444\tA crossed family×seed decomposition assigns at most 0.03 of variance to the replicate-level seed component for all aggregate cells (0.08 for LeVo2’s 4-beat ∆); family and family×seed variation dominate. This is a\n1445\tstability diagnostic on the sentinel, not a claim about an unrestricted seed distribution.\n1446\tA.5.2\n1447\t\n1448\tA.5.2 Off-Attribute Placebos and Genre Breakdown\n1449\t\n1450\tPlacebos rescore the unchanged primary audio: key-family clips with Beat This and beat-family clips with\n1451\tS-KEY. Beat placebo ∆ is the treatment-minus-neutral change in 3- or 4-beat detection; key placebo stability\n1452\tcompares treatment and neutral detected keys by exact and MIREX agreement. Families are retained only\n1453\twhen all three clips are scorable (none were dropped). Intervals use 10,000 genre-stratified family-bootstrap\n1454\tdraws. The main paper reports the resulting contrasts.\n1455\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n1456\t\n1457\t15\n1458\t\n1459\t\fTable A.2: First-pass exact ∆ by genre. Genre cells are descriptive because genre and instrumentation\n1460\tco-vary.\n1461\t\n1462\tSystem\n1463\tACEStep\n1464\t1.5\n1465\tStable\n1466\tAudio\n1467\t3\n1468\tLeVo2\n1469\tMust.\n1470\t(aux.)\n1471\t\n1472\tA.6\n1473\t\n1474\tKey:\n1475\tclassical\n1476\t\n1477\tKey: jazz\n1478\t\n1479\tKey: pop\n1480\t\n1481\tKey: rock\n1482\t\n1483\tBeat:\n1484\tclassical\n1485\t\n1486\tBeat:\n1487\tjazz\n1488\t\n1489\tBeat: pop\n1490\t\n1491\tBeat:\n1492\trock\n1493\t\n1494\t0.625\n1495\t\n1496\t0.573\n1497\t\n1498\t0.563\n1499\t\n1500\t0.688\n1501\t\n1502\t0.156\n1503\t\n1504\t0.313\n1505\t\n1506\t0.031\n1507\t\n1508\t0.094\n1509\t\n1510\t0.729\n1511\t\n1512\t0.635\n1513\t\n1514\t0.698\n1515\t\n1516\t0.521\n1517\t\n1518\t−0.094\n1519\t\n1520\t0.125\n1521\t\n1522\t0.000\n1523\t\n1524\t0.094\n1525\t\n1526\t0.021\n1527\t0.729\n1528\t\n1529\t0.031\n1530\t0.583\n1531\t\n1532\t0.042\n1533\t0.510\n1534\t\n1535\t0.010\n1536\t0.542\n1537\t\n1538\t−0.094\n1539\t0.125\n1540\t\n1541\t0.000\n1542\t0.094\n1543\t\n1544\t0.000\n1545\t−0.031\n1546\t\n1547\t−0.031\n1548\t0.156\n1549\t\n1550\tA.6 Reproduction Package\n1551\t\n1552\tEach primary run contains 768 rows in cases.jsonl, generation_manifest.jsonl, eval_pairs.jsonl,\n1553\tand detections.jsonl, plus config.json and report_v2.json. The executable package follows five\n1554\tstages:\n1555\tStage\n1556\t\n1557\tOperation\n1558\t\n1559\tOutput\n1560\t\n1561\tvalidate\n1562\t\n1563\tResolve model/recognizer assets,\n1564\tenvironment, and canonical cases\n1565\tRender frozen adapters and\n1566\tgenerate neutral/A/B with the\n1567\tfamily seed\n1568\tApply the evaluation window,\n1569\tinvalid policy, S-KEY, and Beat\n1570\tThis\n1571\tRecompute primary, target-level,\n1572\tpaired-model, sentinel, placebo,\n1573\tand validation analyses\n1574\tCheck counts, complete families,\n1575\tseeds, audio properties, expected\n1576\tfailures, and regression\n1577\tsummaries\n1578\t\n1579\tdependency and case audit\n1580\t\n1581\tgenerate\n1582\tscore\n1583\taggregate\n1584\tverify\n1585\t\n1586\taudio and generation manifest\n1587\t\n1588\tevaluation pairs and detections\n1589\t\n1590\tmachine-readable reports and\n1591\tpaper tables/figures\n1592\tpass/fail report\n1593\t\n1594\tThe package supplies a locked environment, configurations, checkpoint acquisition instructions, a smoke\n1595\ttest, reference-dataset preparation scripts, and an artifact index mapping reported claims to outputs. Stable\n1596\tAudio 3 and LeVo2 record repository and checkpoint provenance. ACE-Step’s historical run records released\n1597\tmodel identifiers and inference settings but lacks a contemporaneous repository/checkpoint pin; Mustango\n1598\trecords its model identifier and scheduler revision. This boundary affects exact historical byte recovery, not\n1599\trerunning the stated benchmark protocol.\n1600\t\n1601\t© 2026 Bilibili Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n1602\t\n1603\t16\n1604\t\n1605\t\fReferences\n1606\tAndrea Agostinelli, Timo I. Denk, Zalán Borsos, Jesse Engel, Mauro Verzetti, Antoine Caillon, Qingqing Huang,\n1607\tAren Jansen, Adam Roberts, Marco Tagliasacchi, Matt Sharifi, Neil Zeghidour, and Christian Frank. MusicLM:\n1608\tGenerating Music From Text, January 2023.\n1609\tJade Copet, Felix Kreuk, Itai Gat, Tal Remez, David Kant, Gabriel Synnaeve, Yossi Adi, and Alexandre\n1610\tDéfossez. Simple and Controllable Music Generation, January 2024.\n1611\tZach Evans, Julian D. Parker, C. J. Carr, Zack Zukowski, Josiah Taylor, and Jordi Pons. Stable Audio Open,\n1612\tJuly 2024.\n1613\tZach Evans, Julian D. Parker, Matthew Rice, C. J. Carr, Zack Zukowski, Josiah Taylor, and Jordi Pons. Stable\n1614\tAudio 3, May 2026.\n1615\tFrancesco Foscarin, Jan Schlüter, and Gerhard Widmer. Beat this! Accurate beat tracking without DBN\n1616\tpostprocessing. In Proceedings of the 25th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference,\n1617\tSan Francisco, CA, USA, 2024. doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2407.21658. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.2\n1618\t1658.\n1619\tJunmin Gong, Yulin Song, Wenxiao Zhao, Sen Wang, Shengyuan Xu, Jing Guo, and Xuerui Yang. ACE-Step\n1620\t1.5: Pushing the Boundaries of Open-Source Music Generation, February 2026.\n1621\tYuexuan Kong, Gabriel Meseguer-Brocal, Vincent Lostanlen, Mathieu Lagrange, and Romain Hennequin.\n1622\tS-KEY: Self-supervised Learning of Major and Minor Keys from Audio. arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.12907,\n1623\t2025. doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2501.12907. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12907.\n1624\tYun-Han Lan, Wen-Yi Hsiao, Hao-Chung Cheng, and Yi-Hsuan Yang. MusiConGen: Rhythm and chord control\n1625\tfor transformer-based text-to-music generation, 2024.\n1626\tShun Lei, Huaicheng Zhang, Dapeng Wu, Yaoxun Xu, Lishi Zuo, Wei Tan, Hangting Chen, Guangzheng Li,\n1627\tJianwei Yu, Zhiyong Wu, and Dong Yu. LeVo 2: Stable and Melodious Song Generation via Hierarchical\n1628\tRepresentation Modeling and Progressive Post-Training, June 2026.\n1629\tCheng Liu, Hui Wang, Jinghua Zhao, Shiwan Zhao, Hui Bu, Xin Xu, Jiaming Zhou, Haoqin Sun, and Yong Qin.\n1630\tMusicEval: A Generative Music Dataset with Expert Ratings for Automatic Text-to-Music Evaluation, March\n1631\t2025.\n1632\tPeiling Lu, Xin Xu, Chenfei Kang, Botao Yu, Chengyi Xing, Xu Tan, and Jiang Bian. MuseCoco: Generating\n1633\tSymbolic Music from Text, May 2023.\n1634\tYinghao Ma, Siyou Li, Juntao Yu, Emmanouil Benetos, and Akira Maezawa. CMI-Bench: A Comprehensive\n1635\tBenchmark for Evaluating Music Instruction Following, June 2025.\n1636\tUgo Marchand, Quentin Fresnel, and Geoffroy Peeters. GTZAN-Rhythm: Extending the GTZAN test-set with\n1637\tbeat, downbeat and swing annotations. In Late-Breaking/Demo Session of the 16th International Society for\n1638\tMusic Information Retrieval Conference, Malaga, Spain, 2015.\n1639\tJan Melechovsky, Zixun Guo, Deepanway Ghosal, Navonil Majumder, Dorien Herremans, and Soujanya Poria.\n1640\tMustango: Toward Controllable Text-to-Music Generation, June 2024.\n1641\tCian O’Brien and Alexander Lerch. Genre-specific key profiles. In Proceedings of the International Computer\n1642\tMusic Conference, Denton, TX, USA, 2015. URL https://musicinformatics.gatech.edu/wp-content\n1643\t_nondefault/uploads/2015/09/O%27Brien_Lerch_2015_Genre-Specific%20Key%20Profiles.pdf.\n1644\tColin Raffel, Brian McFee, Eric J. Humphrey, Justin Salamon, Oriol Nieto, Dawen Liang, and Daniel P. W.\n1645\tEllis. mir_eval: A Transparent Implementation of Common MIR Metrics. In Proceedings of the 15th\n1646\tInternational Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference, pages 367–372, Taipei, Taiwan, 2014. URL\n1647\thttps://archives.ismir.net/ismir2014/poster/000039.pdf.\n1648\tMarco Tulio Ribeiro, Tongshuang Wu, Carlos Guestrin, and Sameer S